@flexiarg contracts/declare-the-reductions
@title Declare What The Runtime Does Not Preserve
@keywords reduction, approximation, log-space, tie-break, strong-Kleene, closed-world
@audience contract authors, reviewers, readers of certificates
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; owner strings in MachineContracts.lean; ALIGNMENT "the holder must describe the code accurately")
@references [contracts/holder-states-the-claim translation/declare-what-is-lost]
@see-also pattern-interpretation/compile-guards-exactly
@why a runtime never computes a Lean declaration bit for bit: floats replace reals, a tie-break picks one maximiser, a fact map is three-valued where the Lean state is closed. Undeclared, these differences are either invisible or later mistaken for bugs.
@how the entry's owner string states every reduction: the numeric carrier, choices the Lean leaves open, conditions under which agreement holds, and gaps between the formal object and the source equation. Reductions found by review are declared before the entry closes.
@violation-signature "computes eq. X" for a Double log-sum-exp of an ENNReal definition; a tie-break unmentioned; agreement tests that fix a closed world without saying so.
! conclusion: Each entry lists what the runtime does not preserve and under which conditions agreement holds.

  + context: Lean definitions and Clojure implementations serve different purposes and carriers.

  + IF:
    A correspondence is recorded.

  + HOWEVER:
    Stating differences feels like weakening the claim.

  + THEN:
    Declare them; the claim is the declaration plus the rest.

  + BECAUSE:
    A translation that preserves some aspects and not others is fine as long as a reader can see which.

  + evidence: selection posterior "Double log-space with log-sum-exp, not exact ENNReal"; bayes-choice ascending-name tie-break; acting-order strong Kleene, agreement only when q0 assigns every token (r12); exact belief reductions (a)–(h), including "stored belief = trajectory marginal is true but not formalised".
